2. Permissions

Subject to these terms, each Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive,
royalty-free copyright license to use, run, inspect, reproduce, and modify the
Covered Software, and to distribute it without charge as described in section
4. These permissions include:

(a) personal use, research, teaching, and internal business use;
(b) paid professional work for clients, including digital forensics, incident
    response, consulting, and managed investigations, using the Covered
    Software as a tool operated by You or personnel acting on Your behalf;
(c) charging for and delivering reports, findings, evidence exports, and other
    investigation results produced using the Covered Software; and
(d) paid training, installation, configuration, and support for a recipient's
    own permitted installation, where the charge is for the service and is not
    a condition for obtaining a copy of or access to the Covered Software.

You may run the Covered Software on infrastructure operated by a provider for
Your own permitted use. Fees for general-purpose infrastructure are allowed.
Operating investigations for clients is allowed whether performed on Your
infrastructure or on theirs.

3. Activities requiring separate permission

Without a separate written license from the relevant rights holders, You may
not, directly or indirectly:

(a) sell, rent, lease, or charge a license fee for the Covered Software or a
    modified version;
(b) charge for distributing or supplying a copy, including by bundling it with
    a paid product or a mandatory paid service; or
(c) offer third parties paid access to the Covered Software's functionality as
    a hosted application, subscription, API, or other software service,
    including through a wrapper or a renamed or modified version.

"Charge" includes money or other required consideration. An optional donation
that does not condition delivery, access, or functionality is permitted.
Renaming the software or describing an access fee as a support fee does not
avoid these conditions.

The paid professional work allowed in section 2 remains permitted. A client
paying for an investigation and receiving its results is not purchasing access
to the software. A client paying to operate the software, submit self-service
analysis jobs, or invoke its functionality through an API is purchasing access.
A portal that only delivers investigation results is permitted.

